1201	 
1202	 ## The sea
1203	 
1204	-- **Map format and pipeline.** The map is authored in Tiled as `assets/source/maps/slice-sea.tmj`, in a documented subset of Tiled's JSON format (`packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`): orthogonal, finite, 16 px tiles; one embedded tileset whose tiles carry a `type` of `sea`, `land`, `shoal` or `dock` (placeholder art, so the tileset has no image); a `terrain` tile layer as a plain gid array; an `objects` layer with a named `island` rectangle per island (property `theme`) and a `spawn` point per slot (properties `slot` and `facing`). Anything outside the subset is refused. `npm run map:build` (`scripts/build-map.ts`) converts it into `packages/content/src/maps/sliceSea.ts`: one string of terrain characters per row plus the islands and spawn slots. It refuses a map that fails the validators, and a unit test fails when the module is out of date with the source map (it reads the `.tmj` through a Vite `?raw` import, so the content package stays free of Node APIs).
1205	-- **Validators.** `validateSeaMap` checks §2 and §3 on the decoded map: four small islands, each land tile inside one island rectangle, every island with a dock beside its land and open sea; shoals; eight spawn slots on open sea outside the islands' bounding box and pairwise beyond `ENGAGEMENT_RANGE_TILES` (6); every dock and slot reachable by sea; and, by eight-way shortest paths that never cut a blocked corner, at full speed: each island's nearest neighbour 20–40 s away, no dock-or-slot crossing over 90 s, and a dock within 40 s of every slot. The slice sea is 224 × 208 tiles: neighbours are 22–23 s apart and the longest crossing is 54 s.
1206	-- **Sailing.** Positions are integer sub-units (256 per tile); headings are 64 directions, 0 east and clockwise because y grows down. Unit vectors come from a literal quarter-turn cosine table, so no transcendental function runs. Each world step the throttle (`-moveY`) accelerates towards 64 sub-units a step (five tiles a second), brakes, or reverses slowly from a stop; `moveX` turns one heading a step; no throttle coasts to a stop. The hull is a square of half-size 80 around the position. A move sweeps each axis in turn through the tile columns or rows its leading edge enters and stops flush at the first land or shoal (or the map edge), so a ship slides along an obstacle it meets at an angle, runs aground head-on, and cannot tunnel at any speed.
